  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe center of a sprawling high pressure system will continue to\nmeander southward through the region today offering a \ncontinuation of downright pleasant mid-July weather. Amidst \nlittle to no wind, temperatures today will rise into the mid to \nlocally upper 70s. A lake breeze will push inland this afternoon\nthough the limited marine/land airmass temperature difference \nwill similarly limit the degree of cooling behind the boundary. \nA diurnal cumulus cloud deck will develop by mid-afteroon and \nfade this evening and humidity levels will remain quite \ncomfortable (dew points in the upper 40s to lower 50s). All in \nall, today will be a \"Chamber of Commerce\" type day.\n\nTonight into tomorrow morning, a series of weak upper-level \nshort- wavelength troughs will ripple through the middle \nMississippi River Valley. Dry low-level air will limit the \nopportunity for any rainfall with either ripple in our area. \nWith that said, an increase in mid- level clouds will hold low \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 50s. Friday and Saturday look \nlike gorgeous days as well with highs around 80, continued \nmodest humidity levels, and south to southwesterly breezes. \n\nSunday onward, aggregate troughing will develop along the \nPacific Coast enabling expansive upper-level ridging to expand \nacross the northern United States. A quintessential summertime \npattern is hence expected to set-up with an eastward-expanding \ndome of hot air. The heat will arrive to our local area on \nSunday and continue into Monday with 850mb temperatures rising \ninto the +21 to +23C range supporting highs in the lower 90s. \nHumidity levels will also be on the rise with \nevapotranspirationally-enhanced dew points rising into the mid \n70s (if not higher). As a result, Sunday and Monday already have\nour attention for potential heat headlines across the area. \n\nSunday into Monday also continue to have our attention for a \nthreat for severe weather and flash flooding in the broad\nregion. Conceptually, the overlap of stout northwesterly flow \nand steep mid-level lapse rates on the eastern edge of the \nupper-level ridge atop moisture-laden instability across the \nGreat Lakes will be supportive of quintessential summertime \ndestructive wind-producing thunderstorm clusters. Ensemble \nguidance already exhibits fair consistency in showing a notable \nshortwave (originating from aggregate Pacific troughing to the \nwest) shedding eastward and arriving in the Great Lakes on \nMonday, which may offer the peak coverage of severe weather in \nthe region. Whether or not our local area is impacted by the \nupcoming MCS train or we just stay baking in the heat is \nsomething we'll assess as we get closer. Nevertheless, did touch\nbase with SPC to collaborate on the introduction of severe \nweather probabilities in the broad Great Lakes region for Sunday\nand Monday. \n\nBorchardt",
